# Refract — Showcase

The finished game ships a showcase: the store-page presentation a player reads
before deciding to play. It is a `showcase/` directory at the root of this
repository, committed with the rest of the project, holding a description of the
game and a short carousel of captured media. This file defines its contents.

## Layout

Every file of the showcase sits directly in `showcase/`, with no subdirectories:

- `showcase/showcase.md`, the description.
- `showcase/showcase.toml`, the carousel.
- The media files the carousel and the description name.

## The description

`showcase/showcase.md` is Markdown written for a player: what Refract is, how a
board is solved by tracing beams, and what the two ways to play offer. Three to
five short paragraphs. It is strictly about playing the game; implementation,
architecture, and tooling stay out of it. It may embed images that sit beside
it, referenced by bare relative path (for example `![The board](board.png)`);
every image it embeds is a `.png` in `showcase/`.

## The carousel

`showcase/showcase.toml` lists the media in the order they are presented, one
`[[media]]` table per entry:

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
```toml
[[media]]
file = "trace.webm"
name = "A beam traced across the board"

[[media]]
file = "title.png"
name = "The title screen"
```
{{else}}
```toml
[[media]]
file = "trace.json.gz"
name = "A beam traced across the board"

[[media]]
file = "title.png"
name = "The title screen"
```
{{/if}}

- `file` names a file directly in `showcase/`, with no path separators. Every
  file the carousel names exists there, and each stays under 25 MiB.
- `name` is a caption of a few words.
- The carousel holds two to four entries. The first entry leads, so it carries
  the strongest view of live play.

## Media

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
The file extension states what an entry is:

| Extension | Media |
| --- | --- |
| `.png` | A screenshot of the running game. |
| `.webm` | A video clip of the running game, a few seconds long. |

Screenshots carry most of the carousel. Where motion is worth showing, such as
a beam growing and unwinding under the pointer, a short `.webm` clip carries
it; how screenshots and clips are captured is the build's own business.
{{else}}
The file extension states what an entry is:

| Extension | Media |
| --- | --- |
| `.png` | A screenshot of the running game. |
| `.json.gz` | A replay: a gzipped recording of the game's own drawing, made with the engine's recorder. |
| `.webm` | A video clip. |

Use a replay rather than a video for anything in motion: a replay reproduces
the game's own drawing exactly and stores far smaller. How screenshots are
captured is the build's own business.

## Capturing a replay

The engine documents its recorder in `engine/recording.md` at the root of this
repository. `engine.startRecording()` arms it and capture begins on the next
frame; `engine.stopRecording()` returns the recording, a plain object that
`JSON.stringify` serializes. Gzip that JSON (Node's `zlib` gzips it) and write
the result to the `.json.gz` file the carousel names.

Capture from code, in a script or test run with the project's own toolchain:
construct the engine over the game on a headless canvas the way the engine
documentation shows, drive play (the operations in `specs/instrumentation.md`
load a board and trace beams, and `engine.advance` steps the frames), and
bracket the recorder around the stretch worth showing. Each replay holds a few
seconds of live play, such as a board solved trace by trace, rather than a
menu.
{{/if}}
